%[text] ## Background Knowledge
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_7971] This script expects you to know the basics of matrices. If you are not comfortable with representing a system of equations in matrix form or computing [eigenvalues](internal:M_6ecb) and [eigenvectors](internal:M_7485), please start by working through the [Matrix Methods of Linear Algebra](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/94730-matrix-methods-of-linear-algebra). There are also a few facts about exponentials that will prove relevant. First, the fact that the exponential function is never $0${"altText":"0"}. Second, it is helpful to recognize that complex exponentials are related to sines and cosines by Euler's identity: $e^{i\\theta} = \\cos(\\theta) + i\\sin(\\theta)${"altText":"e^{i\theta} = \cos(\theta) + i\sin(\theta)"}, and thus are bounded in magnitude. 
%[text] ## Systems of Linear [ODE](internal:M_4894)s
%[text] ### Matrix Form of Systems
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_764e] A system of [linear](internal:M_76ba), [homogeneous](internal:M_2eff), [first-order](internal:M_94a7) differential equations can be written in the following form:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dx\_1}{dt} = a\_{11}x\_1 + a\_{12}x\_2 + \\cdots + a\_{1n}x\_n${"altText":"dx1\/dt = a11x1 + a12x2 + ... + a1n\*xn"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dx\_2}{dt} = a\_{21}x\_1 + a\_{22}x\_2 + \\cdots + a\_{2n}x\_n${"altText":"dx2\/dt = a21x1 + a22x2 + ... + a2n\*xn"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\vdots${"altText":"..."}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dx\_n}{dt} = a\_{n1}x\_1 + a\_{n2}x\_2 + \\cdots + a\_{nn}x\_n${"altText":"dxn\/dt = an1x1 + an2x2 + ... + ann\*xn"}
%[text] This system can be written more compactly in **matrix form**:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{d\\mathbf{x}}{dt} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}${"altText":"dx\/dt = A\*x"}
%[text] where $\\mathbf{x} = \\left(\\matrix{ x\_1 \\cr x\_2 \\cr \\vdots \\cr x\_n }\\right)${"altText":"\mathbf{x} = \left(\matrix{ x\_1 \cr x\_2 \cr \vdots \cr x\_n }\right)"} and $\\mathbf{A} = \\left(\\matrix{ a\_{11} & a\_{12} & \\cdots & a\_{1n} \\cr a\_{21} & a\_{22} & \\cdots & a\_{2n} \\cr \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots \\cr a\_{n1} & a\_{n2} & \\cdots & a\_{nn}}\\right)${"altText":"A = \matrix{ a\_{11} & a\_{12} & \cdots & a\_{1n} \cr a\_{21} & a\_{22} & \cdots & a\_{2n} \cr \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \cr a\_{n1} & a\_{n2} & \cdots & a\_{nn}}"}.
%%
%[text] ### Solution Method Using Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors
%[text] To solve the system $\\frac{d\\mathbf{x}}{dt} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}${"altText":"\frac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}"}, we look for solutions of the form $\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t}${"altText":"\mathbf{x}(t) = \mathbf{v}e^{\lambda t}"}, where $\\lambda${"altText":"\lambda"} is a scalar and $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}"} is a vector.
%[text] Substituting this into our differential equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{d}{dt}(\\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t}) = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t}${"altText":"d\/dt(ve^(lambdat)) = Ave^(lambda\*t)"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\lambda\\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t}${"altText":"lambda v e^(lambda t) = A\*v\*e^(lambda t)"}
%[text] Dividing by $e^{\\lambda t}${"altText":"e^{\lambda t}"} (which is never zero), we get:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\lambda\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"lambda\*v = A\*v"}
%[text] This is the **eigenvalue equation**! The values $\\lambda${"altText":"\lambda"} are the **eigenvalues** of matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"\mathbf{A}"}, and the corresponding vectors $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}"} are the **eigenvectors**. Following the normal methods to solve for eigenvalues and eigenvectors, we look for solutions to 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\left(\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda \\mathbf{I}\\right)v = 0${"altText":"\left(\mathbf{A}-\lambda \mathbf{I}\right)v = 0"}    or, equivalently,      $(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = 0${"altText":"(\mathbf{A} - \lambda \mathbf{I}) = 0"}      or        $\\text{det}(\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda\\mathbf{I}) = 0${"altText":"\text{det}(\mathbf{A}-\lambda\mathbf{I}) = 0"}
%[text] where $\\mathbf{I}${"altText":"\mathbf{I}"} is the $n\\times n${"altText":"n\times n"} identity matrix.
%[text] Let $\\lambda\_i${"altText":"\lambda\_i"} be the $i^{\\text{th}}${"altText":"i^{\text{th}}"} eigenvalue with corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}\_i${"altText":"\mathbf{v}\_i"}. Then, each function $\\mathbf{x}\_i(t) = \\mathbf{v}\_ie^{\\lambda\_i t}${"altText":"\mathbf{x}\_i(t) = \mathbf{v}\_ie^{\lambda\_i t}"}is a fundamental solution to our system of equations, and the general solution is a linear combination of all of these fundamental solutions:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{x}(t) = c\_1\\mathbf{v}\_1e^{\\lambda\_1 t} + c\_2\\mathbf{v}\_2e^{\\lambda\_2 t} + \\cdots + c\_n\\mathbf{v}\_ne^{\\lambda\_n t}${"altText":"x(t) = c1 v1 e^(lambda\_1\*t) + c2 v2 e^(lambda\_2\*t) + ... + cn vn e^(lambda\_n\*t)"}
%%
%[text] ### Example: Real 2×2 System
%[text] Consider the following system of differential equations:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dx}{dt} = x + 2y${"altText":"dx\/dt = x + 2y"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dy}{dt} = 4x + 3y${"altText":"dy\/dt = 4x + 3y"}
%[text] First, we write this in matrix form with $\\mathbf{A} = \\left(\\matrix{ 1 & 2 \\cr 4 & 3 }\\right)${"altText":"\mathbf{A} = \left(\matrix{ 1 & 2 \cr 4 & 3 }\right)"}.
%[text] Next, we find the eigenvalues by solving $\\det(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda\\mathbf{I}) = 0${"altText":"det(A-lamba\*I)=0"}:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\det \\left(\\matrix{ 1-\\lambda & 2 \\cr 4 & 3-\\lambda}\\right) = (1-\\lambda)(3-\\lambda) - 8 = 0${"altText":"det(\[1-lambda, 2; 4, 3-lambda\]) = (1-lambda)(3-lambda) - 8 = 0"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda - 5 = 0${"altText":"lambda^2 - 4\*lambda - 5 = 0"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $(\\lambda - 5)(\\lambda + 1) = 0${"altText":"(lambda - 5)(lambda + 1) = 0"}
%[text] So $\\lambda\_1 = 5${"altText":"\lambda\_1 = 5"} and $\\lambda\_2 = -1${"altText":"\lambda\_2 = -1"}.
%[text] For each eigenvalue, we find the corresponding eigenvector by solving $(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda\\mathbf{I})\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}${"altText":"(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\mathbf{I})\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}"}.
%[text] The general solution is: $\\mathbf{x}(t) = c\_1\\mathbf{v}\_1e^{5t} + c\_2\\mathbf{v}\_2e^{-t}${"altText":"\mathbf{x}(t) = c\_1\mathbf{v}\_1e^{5t} + c\_2\mathbf{v}\_2e^{-t}"}, where $\\mathbf{v}\_1 = \\left(\\matrix{-1 \\cr 1}\\right)${"altText":"v\_1 = \[-1 1\]"}, $\\mathbf{v}\_2 = \\left(\\matrix{1 \\cr 2}\\right)${"altText":"v\_2 = \[1; 2\]"}, and $c\_1${"altText":"c\_1"} and $c\_2${"altText":"c\_2"} are arbitrary constants.
%[text] ### Example: Complex 2×2 System
%[text] Consider the following system of differential equations:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 3x - 2y${"altText":"dx\/dt = 3x - 2y"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dy}{dt} = x + y${"altText":"dy\/dt = x + y"} 
%[text] This system can be written as 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\displaystyle \\left(\\matrix{\\frac{dx}{dt} \\cr \\frac{dy}{dt}}\\right) = \\left(\\matrix{3 \~\~\~-2 \\cr 1 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~1}\\right)\\left(\\matrix{x \\cr y}\\right)${"altText":"\displaystyle \left(\matrix{\frac{dx}{dt} \cr \frac{dy}{dt}}\right) = \left(\matrix{3 \~\~\~-2 \cr 1 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~1}\right)\left(\matrix{x \cr y}\right)"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\displaystyle \\text{det}\\left(\\matrix{3-\\lambda \~\~\~-2 \\cr 1 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~1-\\lambda}\\right) = (3-\\lambda)(1-\\lambda)-(-2)(1) = 0${"altText":"\displaystyle \text{det}\left(\matrix{3-\lambda \~\~\~-2 \cr 1 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~1-\lambda}\right) = (3-\lambda)(1-\lambda)-(-2)(1) = 0"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\displaystyle \\lambda^2-4\\lambda+5 = 0${"altText":"\displaystyle \lambda^2-4\lambda+5 = 0"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\displaystyle \\lambda = \\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16-4(5)}}{2} = 2\\pm i${"altText":"\displaystyle \lambda = \frac{4\pm \sqrt{16-4(5)}}{2} = 2\pm i"}
%[text] Solving for the eigenvectors using $(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda\\mathbf{I})\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}${"altText":"(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\mathbf{I})\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}"}, we can see that the general solution is: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{x}(t) = c\_1\\mathbf{v}\_1e^{(2+i)t} + c\_2\\mathbf{v}\_2e^{(2-i)t} = \\left(c\_1\\mathbf{v}\_1e^{it}+c\_2\\mathbf{v}\_2e^{-it}\\right)e^{2t}${"altText":"\mathbf{x}(t) = c\_1\mathbf{v}\_1e^{(2+i)t} + c\_2\mathbf{v}\_2e^{(2-i)t} = \left(c\_1\mathbf{v}\_1e^{it}+c\_2\mathbf{v}\_2e^{-it}\right)e^{2t}"},
%[text] where $\\mathbf{v}\_1 = \\left(\\matrix{2 \\cr 1-i}\\right)${"altText":"v\_1 = \[2; 1-i\]"} and $\\mathbf{v}\_2 = \\left(\\matrix{2 \\cr 1+i}\\right)${"altText":"v\_2 = \[2; 1+i\]"} and $c\_1${"altText":"c\_1"} and $c\_2${"altText":"c\_2"} are arbitrary constants. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_8001] ### Phase Portraits
%[text] A **phase portrait** is a graphical representation of a system of ODEs that shows the directions and shapes of trajectories of its solution in the [phase plane](internal:M_5469). It provides a visual summary of how the system evolves over time for different initial conditions, providing a visual representation of whether a system is stable, unstable, or conditionally stable around each equilibrium point. These ideas are explored in more detail in the [Qualitative Analysis of ODEs](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/95513-qualitative-analysis-of-odes), but phase plots of solutions will be provided in this script as well. 
%%
