%[text] ## Glossary
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_6ecb] **eigenvalue:** For a matrix $M${"altText":"M"}, a number, $\\lambda${"altText":"\lambda"}, is an eigenvalue of $M${"altText":"M"} if there exists a nonzero vector $v${"altText":"v"} with $Mv=\\lambda v${"altText":"Mv=\lambda v"}. Learn more in [Matrix Methods of Linear Algebra](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/94730-matrix-methods-of-linear-algebra).    [Return to text ↑](internal:M_7971)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_7485] **eigenvector:** For a matrix $M${"altText":"M"}, a nonzero vector $v${"altText":"v"} is an eigenvector of $M${"altText":"M"} if there is a number $\\lambda${"altText":"\lambda"} such that $Mv=\\lambda v${"altText":"Mv=\lambda v"}. Learn more in [Matrix Methods of Linear Algebra](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/94730-matrix-methods-of-linear-algebra).    [Return to text ↑](internal:M_7971)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_4894] **ODE:** An [ordinary differential equation](file:./Classification.m:H_9227), e.g. $\\frac{d^2y}{dt^2} - 3t\\frac{dy}{dt} + \\cos(y) = t+3${"altText":"\frac{d^2y}{dt^2} - 3t\frac{dy}{dt} + \cos(y) = t+3"}, is an equation involving total derivatives of the dependent variable. [Return to text ↑](internal:M_7971)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_76ba] **linear:** A differential equation is [linear](file:./Classification.m:H_7e4f) if it can be written in the following form: $f\_n(t)\\cdot \\frac{d^ny}{dt^n}+ f\_{n-1}(t)\\cdot \\frac{d^{n-1}y}{dt^{n-1}} + ...  + f\_{1}(t)\\frac{dy}{dt}+ f\_0(t)\\cdot y = g(t)${"altText":"f\_n(t)\cdot \frac{d^ny}{dt^n}+ f\_{n-1}(t)\cdot \frac{d^{n-1}y}{dt^{n-1}} + ...  + f\_{1}(t)\frac{dy}{dt}+ f\_0(t)\cdot y = g(t)"} [Return to text ↑](internal:M_764e)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_2eff] **homogeneous:** A differential equation is [homogeneous](file:./Classification.m:H_2024) if all terms involve the dependent variable.  [Return to text ↑](internal:M_764e)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_94a7] **first-order:** A differential equation is [first-order](file:./Classification.m:H_7237) if the highest derivative present is first-order.  [Return to text ↑](internal:M_764e)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_5469] **phase plane:** An n-dimensional plot where each point represents the possible state of an n-dimensional dynamical system. Learn more in [Qualitative Analysis of ODEs](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/95513-qualitative-analysis-of-odes).     [Return to text ↑](internal:M_8001)
